/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:135:| Key | Who holds it | What it seals | Where it rests | What unlock needs | Removal and recovery |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:136:| ''Face custody key (FCK)'' | One PersonaGroup's admitted, unlocked face session; on browser, only the authorized tab's sealer holds bare key material | That face's custody rows: root, Keyhive/Veil archive, face proof/member and recovery material, face-private roster/book/join/outbox partitions. It does not replace per-bag content keys (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). | Fresh random key, wrapped only in that face's independent slot/recovery tree; bare only during authorized live use. Never wrapped by S key, a node S key, another face, or a common Keyhive archive. | That face's passphrase, paper route, or measured PRF route; each browser tab asks for its own face unlock. A node face act separately proves its face. | Removing a face revokes its slots/admission and retires its local rows without rotating siblings; old offline bundles and already learned plaintext remain readable to their holders. A one-face recovery route restores only that face's FCK and rows. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:138:The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or guardian-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:149:''LEAD target wire format:'' each D seal authenticates one canonical context containing a format/domain name and version, vessel or store namespace, immutable PersonaGroup ID, row name, row kind, and the complete logical row reference. Encode fields unambiguously as length-prefixed bytes, analogous to the sealed value context at `sealed-store.mem:85-106`. Supply the expected context from the authenticated row selector on open; never trust a ciphertext's claimed face. A carrier copied into another face's slot, a different row name, or a different kind fails authentication. F and S use distinct domain names; an S row binds its named node principal and row kind. New names/formats refuse old ciphertext rather than treating it as clear. ''LEAD:'' exact byte layout and migration version receive implementation review before K15.6; these fields and refusal behavior are normative now.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:160:A bundle for PersonaGroup X contains X's root/identity, X's Keyhive and Veil archive, X's selected proof/recovery keys and rows, X's guardian set/commitments, intended wrapped FCK recovery route, X's selected private roster/book lines, and X's content-key memory section and eligible history. It is sealed under the bundle key and its own route (`sealed-store.mem:68`; `vault-carriers.ts:123-165,301-342,377-397`). It never contains a bare FCK, another face's slot or row, another face's identifiers or content keys, a node place signer, a node S key, guardian-held secret shares, or local-only vessel seat/outbox state. `bundle: stays` remains local unless an explicit per-face recovery rule supersedes it. Restore X into a fresh vessel without opening Y, and refuse an ambiguous old mixed archive. Restore does not mint authority over a sibling.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:169:# ''Isolation red, every row:'' found A and B with independently random FCKs/trees, occupied D carriers for both, and F plus any named node S values. Unlock A alone. Bypass routers, sessions, UI and store adapters; try direct authenticated open, occupied-row replacement, delete, rotation, backup and restore on *each* B carrier using every A key (FCK, member, identity, content, outbox, recovery), all floor values, and all node S/S key legacy values. Every B operation refuses cryptographically and leaves each B occupied ciphertext byte-for-byte unchanged. Copy A ciphertext into B's slot and swap row name/kind: authentication fails. A's same-row replacement succeeds. Restore A's bundle on a fresh vessel: B identity, key, archive and identifiers are absent; B stays unopened. Remove A: B and F still work. This red is stronger than owner-route refusal (`stable-founding-road.mem:459`; `sealed-writer.ts:279-304`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:191:''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:218:: ''One child derives today.'' The only call that reaches `holder.derive` is `custodyGateDoor` (`custody-seat.ts`), and its only consumers are `hearthSovereignPin`, `stoodSovereignPin` and the gate composition in `open-node-vessel.ts`: each derives the SOVEREIGN GATE BUNDLE (`GATE_BUNDLE_INFO`) of one Nexus and route. The registry (`ALL_DOMAINS`) declares other domains, and the door derives under any of them, but no other caller derives. The veil, Keyhive signer and hosting key named in the root's header derive from the vessel key, a face's atom or their own rows, never from this root.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:220:: ''Stands:'' `gate-root` rests under the node's S key (the node route), and a derivation under any other domain refuses by name at the door.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:234:* ''The census sorts by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each present use of a vessel seed, on node and browser alike, sorts by the capability it serves: a floor duty goes to the place signer, which a vessel holds only while it stands a floor; an act on a face's behalf (a dial, a join, an enrolment, a sibling channel, a daemon proof) goes to that face's atom; nothing else keeps a device-wide key. The browser's device seed (`open-browser-vessel.ts`: the Keyhive Individual, the oracle proof, daemon auth, sibling enrolment, the group-secret opener) and the node's (`open-node-vessel.ts`) take the same sort.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:235:* ''The seed's other reach narrows to an allowlist.'' The online place signer serves the floor duties the roadmap's place-signer ruling enumerates; every other present use (the daemon's auth, the device-key signer, the internal asks) either names its domain on that allowlist or takes a key of its own.
