101:Rows whose class includes ''LEAD'' block byte movement until their payload and owning principal are proved. Current `face` and `bundle` fields describe filename/export selection only (`vault-carriers.ts:119-165`); they never confer cryptographic separation. The existing S-key rotation sweeps all hot/cold rows (`:64-68,477-510`), so K15 replaces it with per-face and named-S rotation.
135:| Key | Who holds it | What it seals | Where it rests | What unlock needs | Removal and recovery |
136:| ''Face custody key (FCK)'' | One PersonaGroup's admitted, unlocked face session; on browser, only the authorized tab's sealer holds bare key material | That face's custody rows: root, Keyhive/Veil archive, face proof/member and recovery material, face-private roster/book/join/outbox partitions. It does not replace per-bag content keys (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). | Fresh random key, wrapped only in that face's independent slot/recovery tree; bare only during authorized live use. Never wrapped by S key, a node S key, another face, or a common Keyhive archive. | That face's passphrase, paper route, or measured PRF route; each browser tab asks for its own face unlock. A node face act separately proves its face. | Removing a face revokes its slots/admission and retires its local rows without rotating siblings; old offline bundles and already learned plaintext remain readable to their holders. A one-face recovery route restores only that face's FCK and rows. |
138:The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or guardian-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.
151:Generalize `writeSealedCarrier` from `(key, tree, carrier)` to an explicit `(row class, owner, key, matching tree/door, bound carrier context)`. The caller proves that the key opens the *same owner and row class* against its standing slot tree; an S writer proves its named node door; F has no sealed-carrier writer. Preserve the existing exclusive act, absent-or-authentically-opened standing row rule, and refusal without replacement (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-288`). Seal a full caller-supplied replacement with the same context, write a sidecar, read it back byte-identical, authenticate/open it, then rename (`:290-304`). A failed old-row open, class mismatch, tree mismatch, stale/ambiguous migration shape or failed readback leaves the occupied carrier byte-for-byte unchanged. A whole archive replacement is not an implicit read-modify-write. No encrypt-only capability can perform this overwrite proof. The matching-key rule also governs delete and rotation; never use A's key or node S to authenticate B's standing row.
160:A bundle for PersonaGroup X contains X's root/identity, X's Keyhive and Veil archive, X's selected proof/recovery keys and rows, X's guardian set/commitments, intended wrapped FCK recovery route, X's selected private roster/book lines, and X's content-key memory section and eligible history. It is sealed under the bundle key and its own route (`sealed-store.mem:68`; `vault-carriers.ts:123-165,301-342,377-397`). It never contains a bare FCK, another face's slot or row, another face's identifiers or content keys, a node place signer, a node S key, guardian-held secret shares, or local-only vessel seat/outbox state. `bundle: stays` remains local unless an explicit per-face recovery rule supersedes it. Restore X into a fresh vessel without opening Y, and refuse an ambiguous old mixed archive. Restore does not mint authority over a sibling.
169:# ''Isolation red, every row:'' found A and B with independently random FCKs/trees, occupied D carriers for both, and F plus any named node S values. Unlock A alone. Bypass routers, sessions, UI and store adapters; try direct authenticated open, occupied-row replacement, delete, rotation, backup and restore on *each* B carrier using every A key (FCK, member, identity, content, outbox, recovery), all floor values, and all node S/S key legacy values. Every B operation refuses cryptographically and leaves each B occupied ciphertext byte-for-byte unchanged. Copy A ciphertext into B's slot and swap row name/kind: authentication fails. A's same-row replacement succeeds. Restore A's bundle on a fresh vessel: B identity, key, archive and identifiers are absent; B stays unopened. Remove A: B and F still work. This red is stronger than owner-route refusal (`stable-founding-road.mem:459`; `sealed-writer.ts:279-304`).
191:''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.
245:''The serving tree'' (`sk-slots.bin`) stands on its own: the serving key (SK) wraps under its own slots, bound over the founding route with a wrapped key, salt and slot ids of their own, and no other key wraps it. A rotation of the S key moves no byte of it.
247:''The face tree'' (`faces/<fcid>/fk-slots.bin`) wraps that face's FCK under the face's routes. The three trees stay independent whatever route they share: a rotation or unbind on one leaves the others opening (`face-s-independence.test.ts`).
262:# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.

