/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-field-collision-ii.mem:200:* //Self-certification.// Their SCID = `base58btc(multihash(JCS(initial log entry with {SCID} placeholders)))`, verified by re-substituting the placeholder and recomputing; "the SCID segment is immutable for the life of the DID." Ours: `handlePrefixOf = handle-${sha256(key + genesisOwnerSetHash + recoverySetHash)}`, checked at `verifyHandleKel` seq-0. Same self-certifying-genesis shape — ''and ours is strictly better on portability'': their SCID survives a host move but the DID still //carries a web host//; our prefix contains ''no host at all'' (content-addressed over key+owner+recovery), so it is did:webvh's SCID with the `did:web` baggage already removed. Their `portable:true`-only-in-entry-1 + `alsoKnownAs`-on-move is the exit mechanism we get for free.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-field-collision-ii.mem:206:''Where we catch a bug in THEIRS.'' did:webvh makes log validity depend on wall clock: "the versionTime of every entry MUST be strictly greater than the immediately preceding entry's. Equal timestamps MUST be rejected." That is a ''self-asserted-time ordering dependency'' — precisely the class `epoch-binding-surfaces` KILLED and Willow's hostile-clock demonstrates ("a skewed clock weaponizes newer"). Our signed `handleEventBytes` carries ''no time field''; ordering is pure `seq` + `prevEventCid`. We are immune to the hostile-clock class did:webvh built in. Strong vindication of "correct even if all timestamps were zero."
