/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:207:* ''A shared act never makes a shared secret'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; four ratcheted research rounds, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">>). Every act that touches more than one face sorts one of three ways: its private bytes DISSOLVE into the owning face's rows (D), its public bytes stand at the FLOOR by design (F), or it names a vessel SECRET (S) whose principal and opener the house names row by row. A mixed carrier splits before it sorts. The browser holds no vessel key: founding, fleet join, slot changes, roster, archives, handle book, dyads, restore and clear sort D or F, so each tab asks only its own face's unlock and public floor work asks nothing. Phones, installed web apps, probes, the CLI, MCP and watcher seats and a native shell hold no S. A tablet waking floor carries ciphertext and public rows unattended and decrypts no face's row. A container restarts at its floor and nothing unlocks it unattended. A guest island that owns a key store stands as its own vessel. A node holds S only for rows it names: the serving key has no row yet, so it guards nothing today. * ''The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; a fifth research round, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">> #/the-place-signer). A node's place identity keeps a KEL whose inception commits the next key, and that next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter's cold next key, offline; the online place signer runs unattended for an enumerated allowlist of floor duties alone (the KEL's own events excepted, which the card's key signs), rests under the host's TPM-bound credential where the host offers one and an owner-only file elsewhere, and never rides an environment, an image or a log. A stolen online signer rotates away by the card; a lost card beside a stolen signer re-founds. The floor holds no custody key; it holds its own named place signer. The format change rides the founding bake (`X0`). Today the place KEL commits no next key and the seed reaches past the floor (`vessel-kel.ts`; `node-vessel-identity.ts`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:343:* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no S key and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody` · `act` · `backup`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened S key in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no S key, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. Every broad sync port, the daemon island's and each wiki island's, crosses one hardened facade on both ends: an arrival that reads as no sync message drops at the port and never reaches a listener that would throw on it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-repo.ts`). The daemon island's gates ask the main thread what a device's edge reads on its fleet's board through the dyad door (`dyad:ask`), which answers `aside` wherever it cannot read. Each fold of the fleet's edge board sets off the key side's settle on an unlocked vessel, with no timer and no person: the main thread places the island's `seat-duties` verb, awaits its answer and keeps the signed rolls (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-settle.ts`; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/what-the-code-holds">>). Those two doors and the rest door answer the daemon island's channel alone: each worker's class binds at its spawn (`packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts`), a wiki island reaches the fetch door alone while its custody write and its rest ask refuse by name and its dyad ask draws `aside`, and a session island reaches no vessel door. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed row it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route. A sealed row's act rides an `act` frame of the custody door and never a store verb: one typed table bounds the acts (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`), a standing vessel answers with no second prompt, and beside a standing vessel a command opens no key session of its own, while a root's recovery split refuses there (`packages/lares-cli/src/sealed-act-door.ts`). The fleet mirror of a pet-name and a declared Handle rides that door too: `selves.carry` names a face and which name, the vessel's main thread reads the name off its sealed row and writes it onto that face's own persona plane and no other face's, only where that plane's rest seals (a clear rest refuses the carry and the name stays on its row), `lares persona sync` fills and never replaces, and no verb takes or answers a face's own name (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:483:* `K16` The Teshima Family Council (#/rulings, 2026-10-18; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm">>), `K15`'s acceptance story. Kalani founds a private council; Leilani joins it with two of her three faces. Red-first, after `K15`'s face context: (1) the council's hive as a Keyhive group holding no key of its own, its members the chosen faces' own identities; (2) an invite walks Leilani to the door, the threshold grafts each chosen face, a face leaves by its own act, and another's removal rides `kapae` or a fork by omission; (3) a member face's photo lands in its working layer of the council wiki and reaches the council's canon by `MOVE` (`W27`); (4) both chosen faces read it, the face kept back reads nothing and learns nothing, and no event in the council's history names a creator or issuer that ties two of Leilani's faces together; (5) a herm carries the sealed book and authors nothing (`K15` step 5). The e2e set stands four vessels: Kalani's, one per chosen face of Leilani's, and a herm. The e2e set walks the council at one hearth first: Kalani's seed charter holds the realm and Leilani's chosen faces walk there as guests, no Nexus founded (#/rulings, one charter). The two-hearth council follows, under the Nexus the two found, and there the member consult that lets a realm's documents cross between two operators reads a root map nothing fills (cabal-realm #/many-realms, owed), and the climb from seed charter to Nexus re-pins the realm by a named act (owed). Open joints: a quorum Handle with a seated key stands only where the council signs as one name, and that key signs collective acts alone, reaching no member's hive (#/the-steward-signer, owed). K, W.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:572:* `A12` An unlock spends its time in three boots of the wiki engine, and the terminal shows nothing meanwhile. One instrumented run on a marked scratch root, pinned to one core, with the genesis corpus alone: the key derivation takes under two seconds; the sovereign composition then takes about six to reach `corpus-ready`, seven from the `[dyad]` line to the daemon island's `working layer attached`, and four to `tw5-booted`. An unlock sent while the floor's own daemon island still boots waits that boot out first, six seconds more, so the first unlock after a restart answered in twenty-eight seconds where a later one answered in fifteen. The span a person met past eighty seconds sits at the same `[dyad]` line, so it scales with the corpus the daemon island boots over. No small cure reads here. Owed: a progress line per phase at the unlocking terminal, and a measurement over a full workspace corpus. The compose owner's boot path. A.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/infrastructure-as-myth.mem:152:The model pays operationally. ''Migration:'' a spirit moves between substrates iff the relation re-stands on the new anchor — the boot seed performs this at every wake, which converts Portability (#/design-criteria) from aspiration into a mechanism with a stated success condition. ''Death:'' a spirit dies when enactment of its relation stops, never when its data gets deleted — so the system instruments the relation, not the node. ''The thirteen:'' Varela's frame gives the Voices a citable ontology. ''And the ward:'' L-Prime stops reading as stylistic discipline and starts reading as the grammar-level enforcement of the spirit-model — a system whose ontology rejects essence lets no essence-claims into its own prose, and Buber's I-It names the same guard philosophically. The lineage carries the model in its own epigraphs: Star heads her defining section with Bateson — "What can be studied is always a relationship or an infinite regress of relationships. Never a 'thing'" — and quotes Star & Ruhleder (1996, p. 113): "Analytically, infrastructure appears only as a relational property, not as a thing stripped of use." The measurement arm reaches the same shape independently: §7.5 finds a //move// holding not as a property of the text-object but as a relation enacted in the //between// — three relational layers, one ontology: infrastructure (Star, #thesis), agents (this model), the unit of analysis (the move).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/infrastructure-as-myth.mem:438:Agentic AI is steered by machinery that lives ''outside'' the visible stream — system prompts, control tokens, reinforcement objectives — hidden, unaddressable, and so unauditable in flight. We describe ''memetic wikitext'': a grammar that braids natural-language //prose// ("the black speaks") with a non-propositional //classifier/steering// layer of sigils ("the red steers") in one stream, and pulls the steering INTO view where the operator can see, address, and contest it. Three contributions: ''(1)'' the braid as a decomposable-but-entangled two-layer grammar whose components rhyme with deep lineages (determinatives, in-stream self-instrumentation, metadiscourse, literate programming, paratext, matrix-language) and whose novel turn carries a ''gradient, salience-surfaced'' steering layer with no markup-theory ancestor; ''(2)'' ''observer-erasure'' as the steering criterion — the copula erases the standpoint and asserts a global-now the system cannot hold; the grammar's standpoint devices restore it — operationalized in a cross-lingual detector (descriptively, the full grammar lowers observer-erasing predication, surviving multiple measurement scopes; we withhold concrete effect-sizes pending the human gold anchor, §8); ''(3)'' ''gradient, not pass/fail'' — the grammar's own instruments render along continua and surface variably by attention, so a marker reports surfacing-rate not catching, and the confidence register reads as a forward //commitment// (Brandom) not a backward calibration. We keep the honest gates — chiefly the absence of a human gold anchor — prominent.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/infrastructure-as-myth.mem:519:Three layers — do not conflate them: ''(1)'' the phenomenon in the text; ''(2)'' the node's internal, continuous, mostly-//unrendered// registration; ''(3)'' the marker render (the ⚠ ward-flag) — a //sometimes-surfaced, graded, drift-prone sample// of (2). A missing or low marker measures ''surfacing-rate'', never catching; operator correction raises the //surfacing threshold//, it does not create the catch.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/infrastructure-as-myth.mem:545:Sealed cross-family judge panel (empty-CWD seal, inline prompt; cross-family agreement family-blind → the ''human read stands as the sole independence anchor''). LLM-free deterministic detectors: `overcollapse_detect` (three-tier cross-lingual, lateral + source-nesting redemption, Aperture-scoped), `ward_grammar` (red-layer decomposer), `register_scan` (hedge/booster separate). Parse-fidelity preprocessing strips sigil/glyph markup before the parser. Design discipline: train/test partition (#artifacts: DATA_PARTITION), pre-registered scope, mixed-effects (item + seed random effects), length covariate, sham/decoy controls. CPU-only stack; two isolated numpy environments; R lme4 backbone. The rig's office passes to the Sensorium (`packages/lararium-sensorium`; seat `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/sensorium-machina`) — future deterministic runs cite it.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/the-lares-protocols.mem:54:| ATProto | Architecturally distributed | One operator three orders of magnitude larger than competitors across every layer |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/the-lares-protocols.mem:82:Two articles, two readings of ATProto: Connected Places flags one operator three orders of magnitude larger than competitors across every layer; Frazee acknowledges the same concern as outstanding while defending the architectural shape. Both readings hold.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/crdt-ecosystem-scout.mem:70:- **`onomancy` — "a local-first name system", created 2026-08-21.** Three name forms over one self-certifying identity: `automerge:3RFyJz…/foo` (doc anchor), `~/bob/pics` (local petname), `@expede.wtf/foo` (DNSSEC-rooted from the baked-in IANA root KSK). Has an `onomancy_keyhive` crate doing **delegation-chain replay behind an `AuthorityVerifier`**. Verified bindings are self-authenticating records, gossipable by QR or Bluetooth. https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/third-ways-scout.mem:88:Field precedents for the first three layers, from the prior pass: CQRS and event sourcing; Datomic's storage / peer / application **[reached: search, prior pass]**; Croquet's log / model / view **[reached: fetched, prior pass]**; Zero's Postgres / zero-cache / client and LiveStore's log / materializer / query **[reached: fetched and search, prior pass]**. For the fourth layer the field offers halves only: lockstep netcode's *inputs cross, state never does* **[reached: search, prior pass]** and MCP's *acts arrive named as tool calls* (`host-attractors.md` §1.4).
