/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mudlet-scout.mem:97:- **GMCP**: inbound packages land in the global `gmcp` table under their dotted path and raise an event of the same name (`gmcp.Char.Vitals`); outbound `sendGMCP("Package.Name <json>")` [reached: search]. `gmod.enableModule(user, module)` reference-counts modules per user and sends `Core.Supports.Add`; `disableModule` sends `Core.Supports.Remove` when the last user leaves; `reenableModules()` re-sends the list on `sysProtocolEnabled` [reached: fetched]. `Core.Supports.Set` replaces the whole list; names are dotted, version 1 assumed if omitted [reached: search]. 5.0 PTB fixed a `__needMerge` leak in GMCP merge keys [reached: fetched].
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mudlet-scout.mem:149:All four keep the three layers apart: the STORE never learns Mudlet exists; the ENGINE answers `port:ask`; Mudlet is a PROJECTION. The difference is **where the port carrier runs and who owns the telnet socket to the game**.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mudlet-scout.mem:281:The `@lararium/sensorium` manifest describes "the perception machina (content/structure/form/persistence planes, capture, ffz enrichment, worldline, the lares MCP)", reading a pinned mempalace sidecar and never federating. What a Mudlet integration would want from it: a **capture plane** that turns the raw river plus GMCP into typed percepts (room entered, entity appeared, damage taken, speech heard) instead of regexes per game; a **worldline** that orders those percepts in time per character so a spirit's intent reads "what happened in the last three rounds" rather than three screens of text; **form** recognition for ASCII art, tables and prompts; and the structure plane as the source of the room/NPC/quest tiddlers in §4.1 so the Mudlet package stops owning parsing. Deferred per the operator; until then the package and the proxy parse GMCP only and leave text to the player's triggers.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mudlet-scout.mem:307:**Gaia 2026, Telarus on Islands of Myth.** Telarus opens Mudlet 5.0. Ctrl+1 is Islands of Myth through the household Pi's proxy on `localhost:4000`; Ctrl+2 is the hearth, a second profile dialled to `localhost:4001`, where the lararium poses as a game and the `lar.mpackage` has drawn three Geyser panes: the river of journal notes, the open quests filtered by `[tag[MudQuest]field:state[open]]`, and the map pane the proxy fills from `Room.Info` as he walks. He types `#note the ferryman wants a tide-stone` in the game tab; the alias posts `Lar.Write` with a `txId`, the quest pane updates a beat later with the echo named as foam. Two spirits, Haku and Lio, hold the squad grant Telarus signed as a wizard that morning; the mudlib's `Lar.Squad.Announce` module (his own LPC, three evenings' work) tags them `[squad:Telarus]` in `who`. A sea-wolf attacks on the causeway; Haku's reflex table, reading `Char.Vitals` every two-second round, drinks at 40 % without asking anyone; the lararium's intent turn, five seconds later, reads the worldline and says "fall back to the pier, Lio pulls aggro"; the standing order lands as a tiddler and both reflex layers read it next round. Telarus leaves for dinner; the grant's rule stops the squad at the pier, and `squad/causeway/log/2026-10-16` holds every command they sent for the other wizards to read.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/0-method-and-the-honest-limit-on-this-report-52ebe0.mem:369:- **Google Recovery Contacts** (Oct 16, 2025): up to **10 trusted contacts** per account; a locked-out user shares a numeric code; the contact number-matches it against three options; **15-minute expiry**; each person may serve as recovery contact for up to **25 accounts**; unavailable for Workspace and child accounts; Advanced Protection members may help others but not set their own. Google still layers device/location/IP heuristics on top and may still hold the account. Named risk: social-engineering the contact — **which is precisely the §5.2 UI-bound-adversary problem arriving at recovery time.**
