/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/managed-identity-scout.mem:69:> ''Nobody verifies who the manager is.'' None of 40 parental-control apps implemented multi-guardian consent; vendors assert //"only the device owner's consent is required"// — while ''device ownership extends beyond legal guardianship''; and a regulator's own remedy accepts ''an unverified self-attestation checkbox''.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/two-way-interaction-scout.mem:53:1. **TW5 interaction decomposes into three kinds of crossing, and the ruling already names all three.** (a) A *true event*: a click, a key chord, a drop, a file pick, a `tm-*` message. It queues, carries an id, and gets `applied` / `stale-target` / a named refusal. (b) A *sensed snapshot*: a field's whole value, a caret, a checked state, a select's chosen keys, a scroll offset, a geometry measured at the act. It crosses as absolute state with a page revision; the engine never asks for it synchronously. (c) A *write*: a whole record with a `txId`, which the glass engine (full/skinny/story) or a lean host (Mudlet, MCP) sends in place of events. Every TW5 widget sorts into one of these, and the sort decides which layer owns it (§2).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:48:  - The mail does **not** state the old 2003 key was on a compromised box; it presents the new key as part of recovery. *Confidence that the roll was caused by the compromise: high (timing + placement in the recovery mail). Confidence that the old private key was actually exposed: low — never stated.*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:183:**The generalization worth carrying:** across every system examined, package/archive signing is **single-key custody with one signature**. Nobody does threshold *signing*. Where m-of-n appears, it is in one of two adjacent places — (a) **recovering** a lost secret (Debian's 3-of-5 Shamir), or (b) **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian's 2 DD signatures, kernel.org's 2 account-holder signatures, Arch's 3 master signatures). The signing operation itself is always a single online holder, and the recovery path in every incident above was the same shape: *mint a new key, get it signed by humans out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new trust anchor to users through the old one if it still works — or by hand if it doesn't.*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/2-apple-screen-time-partially-reached-e03518.mem:63:Note the load-bearing asymmetry: the press release says the icon is *"only removable by a parent or guardian"*; the Order says the parent may **disable the notice entirely, pre-installation, on a bare self-attestation**. The gate is an unverified checkbox — Section I.A/B requires only "an express written attestation from the purchaser," and the Order simultaneously forbids the vendor from helping: *"Respondents cannot provide purchasers with written attestation language"* and *"cannot suggest, direct, or otherwise assist, purchasers in submitting fraudulent written attestations."* Nothing in the Order requires notice **to the monitored person**; every notice duty runs to the **purchaser** (Home Page Notice, Purchase Page Notice, Section II.A/B). The only monitored-person-facing artifact is the Icon Notice — the one thing a parent may switch off.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/2-apple-screen-time-partially-reached-e03518.mem:105:The only enforceable, quotable rule that the supervised party be *shown* they are managed is the FTC's Icon Notice — and its sole exception is the parent/child case, disabled by unverified purchaser self-attestation before install. Platform documentation (Apple, Microsoft, Google marketing) is written entirely from the supervisor's seat; across every page reached, not one sentence describes what the supervised person sees, is told, or is notified of when settings change. The child-facing surface that *is* documented (Apple's exception request) reveals a limit's existence only by collision with it.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:338:''LEAD — dependency/order:'' per-face custody keys belong *inside K15* because keys, archive/store ownership and one-face restore are all part of that PersonaGroup boundary. F6 slice-three (a′) is the write choreography layered onto the K15 row/key ownership; it cannot manufacture isolation over VK.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:634:''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' K15 owns one identity, store, registry, archive and content-key seat per PersonaGroup; K15 step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`; `keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:55-72`). F6 slice three consumes face context for asker-sealed writes (`stable-founding-road.mem:504`). ''LEAD (confirmed-by-both):'' define the exhaustive D/F/S row schema, independent FCK and slot/recovery tree in K15.1; establish per-face ownership and remove above-face identity delegation in K15.1/.2; bind each tab to one face/session and apply (a′) in K15.3 + F6 slice three, ensuring the selected sealer can open the existing row before replacement; complete face-only removal in K15.4 and one-face recovery/restore in K15.5; move identity bytes, split stores, FCK slots and archives behind one migration fence in K15.6. F6 routes writes over this established key ownership; it cannot grant isolation over a VK. Build floor rows without a vessel key throughout, and make node-only VK contingent on a measured S row. Sources: `../PRIOR-YIN.md:66-89`, `../PRIOR-YANG.md:56-69`, `sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:845:''LEAD sequencing:'' K15.1 define exhaustive row schema and independent FCK/slot/recovery tree; K15.2 enforce per-face identity ownership and split mixed carriers; K15.3 bind browser tab/session and node session to one face, apply (a′), land F6 slice three with authenticated occupied-row replacement; K15.4 remove one face and its rows without sibling rotation; K15.5 one-face recovery/restore; K15.6 migrate identity bytes/stores/FCK routes/archive in one fence. F6 routes writes over established key ownership; it cannot create isolation over VK. Keep Herm's private place signer decision and container no-human restart behavior explicit prerequisites. `sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309` shows current occupied-row replacement authenticates existing ciphertext and verifies before rename; preserve that refusal/atomicity in the face writer.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:948:For every row-bearing node/container type above, acts are filtered from the merged census: (1) found place / VK / slot tree → place signing seed ''S'' under today's implementation; public place/card/charter F; each PersonaGroup root/FCK/slot tree D; serving key/tree only becomes S if a serving payload is actually present; (2) fleet offer/join pending → public signed transport F, target face's pending secret/address/record D; (3) slot bind/remove/recovery → that face's route tree D; (4) roster, labels, declarations, handle-book → face-specific content D, minimal opaque public routing only F; (5) Keyhive/Veil archive, dyads, face proof/member and Nexus keys → D; (6) face backup/restore → D; (7) clear custody removes that face's rows and routes, while F survives; (8) floor card, public charter, public gate and public artifact carriage → F. Prior precise carrier/caller citations: `../PRIOR-YIN.md` census table and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` census table. Node carrier line inventory: `vault-carriers.ts:287-398`; rotation's current VK-carrier sweep: `vault-carriers.ts:477-510` (current mechanic to be replaced by face/row-aware rotation).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1013:For every node/container/seat type in the previous table, common acts filter as follows (''MEASURED current carriers; LEAD target rows''): founding place signing seed is S on node (public verifying half/card/charter F); PersonaGroup root, FCK and slot tree D; fleet public signed offer/answer F while target-face pending secret/address/join record D; face slot changes/recovery D; private roster, labels, handle-book/recognition graph D while minimal public opaque routes F only after leakage review; Keyhive/Veil archives, dyads, proof/member/Nexus keys D; per-face backup/restore D; clear-custody removes only that face's D and leaves F; future serving rows S only when instantiated and named. The carrier/caller evidence appears in `../PRIOR-YIN.md` and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` census tables and `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:287-398`; archive row selector remains unresolved.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1107:''LEAD recommendation: split a long-lived place root from a boot signer, and give the place identifier a KERI-like pre-rotated recovery path.'' The online signer may sign a tightly enumerated set of place acts and public floor-gate derivations. An offline owner-held root or threshold-held next key authorizes rotation of the online signer and publishes a verifiable KEL event. Keep at least one next key outside the always-on node. Use TPM/systemd encrypted credential as an optional at-rest wrapper for the online signer on supported nodes, with explicit fallback policy on systems lacking TPM.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1109:This third shape improves on plain clear-file candidate (1) by making stale online-key theft recoverable and machine-tied at rest where available. It improves on human-at-every-boot candidate (2) by preserving Herm public service and unattended container restart. It does ''not'' erase the theft window: attacker with current signer can forge until verifiers accept the rotation; attacker with next/root key can seize or permanently lose the place identity. If recovery cannot be implemented with independently protected next key, do not claim pre-rotation protection. Root/next compromise in pure KERI can require abandoning the identifier and re-founding identity with ecosystem-level rebinding.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1124:Detection: compare KEL histories / witnessed public floor records, keep tamper-evident receipts and independent public-key pins, and alert on conflicting valid histories or unexpected rotation. Recovery: rotate place key through authenticated KEL pre-rotation if next key remains safe; revoke/replace relying pins and re-found/rebind if recovery authority is also lost. Kapae/re-found semantics are house-specific and were not independently verified in this internet pass, so this mapping is ''LEAD'', not a measured procedure. A stolen current key enables forgery until rotation is accepted; pre-rotation does not retroactively erase that window.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1241:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place KEL offers no recovery: `vessel-kel.ts:2-13` and `node-vessel-identity.ts:185-198` explicitly define non-transferable identity, no next-key commitment, and fresh founding after loss/theft. There is no place KEL rotation path in the assigned source; `rg` found only inception/decode/verify. `lares nexus seal rotate` rotates charter seats/epoch, not the place key. Vault `--rotate` rotates custody VK, not this seed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1242:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' With current non-rotatable KEL, recovery means abandon/re-found the place identity and re-pin/re-carry it through the relevant Nexus, fleet, and floor-gate relationships. The board can detect signatures that fail, but cannot distinguish attacker signatures made with the stolen valid seed from the real place. Kapae can shadow relationships/edges under their own authorities; it does not recover the compromised place identity or revoke every consumer automatically. Re-found carries continuity and coordination costs.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1253:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' A genuinely useful split would put a long-term place root offline and have it certify replaceable boot keys, with explicit expiry/revocation/recovery authority; merely certifying one static boot signer does not help if that signer cannot be revoked. But this changes all verifiers/pins and recovery semantics. A pre-rotation place KEL could make compromise recoverable only if the next private key stays outside the stolen boundary and all consumers enforce the chain.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1254:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' For the existing code contract, prefer a narrowly named place-signer S protected at rest and auto-available for public floor, while explicitly excluding VK/FCK and documenting non-rotatable identity/re-found recovery. This is a decision recommendation, not a claim that code already meets the protection standard.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1272:# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 1: named place signer, protected at rest, auto-loaded for floor only.'' Keep the floor free of custody VK/FCK; name the signer as a place-identity capability, constrain its APIs to the existing place/floor domains, and protect the seed with an owner-only file at minimum or an OS/TPM keystore where it can actually boot unattended. Document the remaining host-compromise risk and mandatory re-found recovery under today's KEL. This best preserves current herm availability, though code currently uses the signer beyond a narrow signing API (including derivation/group-secret opener), so implementation must narrow/justify each of those uses.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1305:| Place KEL has only self-signed inception, no next-key commitment or rotation/recovery path. | ''CONFIRM.'' `vessel-kel.ts:2-13,35-59`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:185-198`; TWIN's source search found only inception/decode/verify. `nexus seal rotate` and VK rotation are different key/authority lanes. Current recovery is abandon/re-found and update dependent pins/relationships (the operational steps are LEAD). | twin-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1343:| ''Signal'' | Signal's key-transparency service uses log/index structures and independent auditors to detect inconsistent views of user key mappings; PQXDH puts user identity/prekeys on server while private keys stay with user. [[Automatic Key Verification|https://signal.org/blog/automatic-key-verification/]], [[PQXDH spec|https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/]]. | Analogy for witnessing public place-key history and keeping face keys separate. Transparency detects equivocation; it does not prevent place-seed theft or itself authorize recovery. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1356:''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' Existing place KEL provides no next-key commitment, rotation, or recovery. Therefore it offers no cryptographic way to distinguish legitimate from attacker signatures made with the same stolen seed. Independent transparency witnesses and authenticated pins could reveal inconsistent public histories, but are not in the measured place KEL.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1367:''Rank 1 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: third shape, split root and boot signer with pre-rotated recovery.'' Keep an owner/threshold-controlled place root and committed next key offline; use a replaceable, narrowly scoped online place signer for unattended floor operations. Optionally protect its at-rest credential with TPM/systemd or OS keystore where available. Publish and verify a KEL-like rotation chain across all consumers. This improves theft recovery while preserving service. It is not a quick wrapper change: existing KEL and relying pins do not support this today, and current signer consumers must be narrowed/audited first.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1369:''Rank 2 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: candidate (1), named floor signer protected at rest.'' Explicitly amend floor law to “no custody VK/FCK; named place signer may be S.” Use owner-only storage at minimum and OS/TPM protection where available; make release policy and unsupported-platform fallback explicit. This aligns with present unattended behavior, but a stolen key currently requires re-found and source shows broader uses than the proposed “place acts only.”
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1387:Run an adversarial no-human boot and copied-disk test. On the authorized host, a faceless Herm and restarted container must publish the expected place card/relay/gate identity; no seed or child secret appears in disk-visible public artifacts, logs, environment, or API. On a copied disk / wrong host, signer release fails if TPM/host binding was selected (or the documented owner-only file exposure is demonstrated if not). With no VK/FCK, attempts to read, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly. Verify allowlisted signer domains only, and specifically assert that group-secret and dyad derivations cannot become broad custody opens. Then simulate current online-key theft: offline pre-rotated authority emits a valid successor; all consumers advance and reject old-key place acts. If offline next/root is also compromised, require re-found rather than claiming recovery. Candidate (2) variant must assert that no human means no signed place floor identity.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1437:''LEAD 2026-10-19:'' Map systemd encrypted credentials/TPM to wrapping the place signer at rest and unattended boot release. This may reduce copied-disk theft while preserving Herm uptime; it does not address compromise of a running host. Vault auto-unseal and Kubernetes secret mounts resemble delegated machine release and have similar runtime trust; plain Kubernetes Secret is not enough. Tor/SSH/WireGuard/ACME show how operators accept an always-on service identity and respond to theft by replacing/rebinding public identity. KERI pre-rotation is the closest recovery design only after adding the absent place-KEL machinery. Signal transparency is useful detection inspiration, not key protection.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1446:''Rank 1 — third shape: split long-lived place root from scoped boot signer, with independently protected pre-rotated successor. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Keep Herm's operational signer online, narrowly authorized for explicitly named floor/place domains. Let an offline owner-held root or threshold process authorize successor rotation; keep next seed off host. Optionally wrap online signer with TPM/systemd credential on supported systems. First remove or separately justify non-place uses (notably dyad veil/face enrollment). This preserves unattended public boot and adds a recovery path; it does not prevent forgery while a current signer remains accepted. Requires new KEL contract, verifier, rotation command, witness/pin update, and recovery runbook.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1459:''Red test (LEAD, 2026-10-19):'' fresh zero-face Herm boot and unattended container restart must serve public card/charter and derive only allowlisted floor-gate pins; no VK/FCK/SK or face row opens. Read copied signer file and show attacker can forge current place assertion, then rotate through separately held next authority and prove old key fails while new KEL verifies; attempted floor-door sovereign derivation and cross-face decrypt must refuse. Repeat on rank 2 without offline rotation to demonstrate expected permanent-key recovery gap; rank 3 must demonstrably refuse signer-backed endpoints before human unlock while leaving public unsigned bytes readable.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:210:- **The negative result, and it is load-bearing:** **the DPS does not specify what happens if a Crypto Officer or a Recovery Key Share Holder becomes unavailable or leaves.** It mentions "Job Rotation Frequency and Sequence" and says only *"Other positions are rotated and replaced as needed."* No succession procedure. **In the world's most audited key ceremony, the "guardian is unreachable" case is unwritten.** And as far as the public record shows, **the 5-of-7 recovery path has never been exercised.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:421:## 3 (REPLACEMENT) — SOCIAL RECOVERY AS PRACTICED
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:457:- 2024-06-09: **the 2FA server itself was compromised.** The attacker *"temporarily replaced affected users' 2FA data with their own email addresses,"* initiated social recovery, received the code, and Loopring's relayer *"signed 'YES' to the social recovery request."* **The attacker did not defeat recovery — they invoked it, exactly as designed.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:480:Launched 2011, relaunched 2013, ~**2 billion accounts**, 3-friend confirmation. **Removed 2022.** Facebook's own help-page notice, verbatim: *"Trusted contacts is going away soon. This means your chosen friends will no longer be able to help you get back on Facebook if you lose access to your account."* Replacement advice: keep your email and phone current. **Meta ran the largest possible experiment in social recovery for ~9 years, published no results, and shut it down.** *(This supersedes my earlier 16/20 — the verbatim notice was retrieved.)*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:486:Matthew Green's [critique](https://blog.cryptographyengineering.com/2020/07/10/a-few-thoughts-about-signals-secure-value-recovery/): *"The key weakness of the SVR approach is that it depends strongly on the security and integrity of SGX execution"* — memory dump → offline dictionary attack whose difficulty rests *"entirely on the strength of a user's password"* (Signal encouraged **4-digit** PINs); retry-limit bypass; attestation-key leakage forcing replication into simulated non-SGX enclaves. On consent: *"My major issue with SVR is consent"* — he calls the rollout a *"dark pattern."*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:608:> **The generalization: nobody does threshold *signing*.** Every archive is signed by one online key. Where m-of-n appears it is in one of two adjacent places — **recovering** a lost secret (Debian 3-of-5), or **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian 2 DDs, kernel.org 2 account holders, Arch 3 master keys). And every recovery in the record has the same shape: **mint a new key, get humans to sign it out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new anchor through the old key if it still works — by hand if it doesn't.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:647:Across five research streams, **not one documented failure in the entire public record turned on Shamir-versus-multisig.** They turned on: quorum counts that weren't counts of distinct signers (four sightings, one a recurrence in the same library six years later); custody that was correlated in ways nobody re-checked (Ronin's 5-of-9 that was 1-of-1, Loopring's guardians-of-guardians, FNMT's access codes stored beside the HSM); ceremonies whose script and reality diverged undetected for six months to six years; guardians who forgot, went dark, or folded to a warm phone call; and vendors who left. **The two forks differ most in a place neither branch names: Fork A's assembled secret is a bearer token that can be couriered, delegated, and improvised with — which is simultaneously its worst security property and the only reason ICANN could hold a ceremony during a pandemic. Fork B cannot bend, in either direction.** And the finding that changes the shape rather than answering it: under KERI's own rules, **a non-delegated rotation cannot supersede another rotation** — so whichever fork you take, a compromise of the recovery authority is terminal unless the identifier is delegated. That is a third axis, and on severity it dominates the one you asked about.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry.mem:38:''The reading that clears it, and it turns on WHOSE key.'' The refusal's own stated reason reads //"self-ownership resolves to no persona head and forecloses recovery"//, and both halves belong to a PERSONA's face. A Herm's owner stands as neither a persona nor the handle key itself: the Herm's ''PLACE DID'' owns it — "a somewhere, not a someone … and never an operator DID" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes">> #/the-identity-class-ladder, quote license), the device-minted vessel key the keel already boots on. And its recovery never wanted a persona head: a Herm "re-establishes from its operator … The contract IS the whole authority relation; re-issuing it IS the recovery" (`identity-classes.mem` #/herm-establishment, quote license). ''The wall holds where it was built and does not reach here'': the refusal bars a face with NO owner, and a Place DID stands as an owner.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry.mem:110:''Signed by the Herm's OWN vessel key, device-minted.'' Not a persona root (the class forbids one, `vessel-standing.ts:69-70`), not the relay gate key (that one derives domain-separated for the transport and stays a transport identity — `carriage-relay.ts:60-77`; joining them would rebuild the link the veil model spends its design preventing, `carriage-relay.ts:62-65`). The ''Place DID'' fills the owner-set: `mintHandleInceptionSet(handleKeyDid, [placeDid], 1, recoverySetHash)` — the same call `mintHandleInception` already sugars for a 1-of-1 (`handle-kel.ts:228-237`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-keybase-1f2d42.mem:124:- This is an explicit, self-acknowledged tension in Signal's own design philosophy: SVR/PIN was purpose-built specifically because private *contact-list* cloud backup requires a key that survives total device loss — the same problem Keybase and Matrix solve with paper keys / recovery keys. All three systems converge on the same shape: **no-single-key marketing, one recoverable human secret in practice, protected by rate-limiting rather than secrecy strength.**
