## ✶ Observe — MEASURED

**Prior art and event shape.** `vessel-kel.ts:1–52` explicitly defines the current place log as one self-signed inception `{t,k,sig}`, no seq, predecessor, next digest, or event CID. `node-vessel-identity.ts:199–228` and `:429–442` require that exact inception and load its private seed directly. This puts KERI’s *non-transferable* one-key model in code today; it cannot rotate. KERI v1.1 models transferable AIDs with hash-linked, sequence-numbered key events and pre-rotation: a rotation reveals the one-time key committed by its predecessor ([KERI overview and pre-rotation](https://trustoverip.github.io/kswg-keri-specification/), sections “AID” and “Key rotation/pre-rotation”).

The house already built reusable patterns: `persona-kel.ts` provides stable prefix, `seq`, `prevEventCid`, rolling next recovery commitment, digest-addressed events and verify-then-fold; `handle-kel.ts` supplies required next-key digest per event, sequence/hash links, reveal verification and key retirement; `cofound.ts`/charter founding prints cold next-key cards with read-back. The place relation differs in authority and event scope, not in causal spine: one place AID remains stable while its operational signer rotates. LEAD: reuse the house’s event envelope/hash/fold primitives, with place-specific event authority and fields. Inception commits next-key digest; rotation carries seq, prior event digest, revealed new key, and a fresh next digest. No interaction event surfaced as necessary: place pin claims bind the KEL head; ordinary floor actions already have their own domains/records. Add interactions only if a concrete place-authority mutation cannot live in the existing duty record.

**Custody, now and target.** `vault-carriers.ts:308–315` assigns `vessel-key` to a node-owned floor row (currently private seed clear in `.vessel-key*.json`) and `vessel-kel` to public F. `custody-holder.ts:2–20` says its S key/root exist only while unlocked; it does not hold the floor signer. `gate-root` and `place-archive` sit hot under S (`vault-carriers.ts:363–366`); neither holds floor-key custody. `K15 custody step zero:32,46–47,105–115,262` gives the target: named place signer stays outside S/FCK, public KEL stays F, online signer runs only the floor allowlist; cold next key rides the founding card offline beside the seed charter’s cold next key. K15 leaves a narrower open joint: signer protection/restart semantics—TPM-bound credential when available, owner-only file otherwise per road:207; neither an S row sealed under its own node key nor operator paper for the *online* key. The cold future key remains paper/card custody.

Road:207 rules unattended signing for only enumerated duties, except KEL events signed by card key; signer never rides environment, image, or log. Theft of online signer rotates away using the precommitted card. Loss of card alongside signer means re-found the place. K15:262 records two alternatives for online-key access but says neither selected; reconcile that stale open joint against the later explicit road:207 ruling: scoped signer unattended, with at-rest protection selected by host capability. Never treat a shared rotation act as a shared secret.

Recovery has layers, not a magic recovery key. `identity-classes.mem:334–375,482–498` rules Reading-B: recovery rotates to a fresh operational key, retires the old and never resurrects it; self-A memory derives an independent key from persona root, while B-name guardian recovery authority also rotates, and C end-user acceptance establishes the recovered human socially. This place lane has no persona-root or guardian authority today: house law says a Herm place re-establishes from its operator (`identity-classes.mem:48,180–184`). LEAD: if cold next key is lost but current signer remains safe, advance by a newly provisioned/re-founded place identity only through a defined owner act; if current signer leaks, use committed cold key. A lost cold card plus leaked current key cannot securely recover that same AID; re-found, re-carriage/re-vouch, preserve old evidence as history. The exact re-establishment ceremony and how B-name recovery authority enters a *place* relation remain open; do not silently borrow face recovery.

**Five scoped duties.** `place-signer.ts:38–90` and `open-node-vessel.ts:1497–1507` measure the current list and sole node wiring. Domains: `carriage-carrier` (“I carry for this Nexus” seal); `place-floor-pin` (pin claim); `flow-pointer` (served pointer); `raise-challenge` (waking-floor challenge); `gate-place-finish` (place finish when dialing another place’s gate). Matchers: canonical JSON exact leading `kind` for carrier; JSON sorted head `digest,kind` for floor pin; `FLOW_POINTER_DOMAIN|` joined string for pointer; JSON `epoch,kind` for raise; gate finish already begins u32 length + domain. These matchers only route/check existing preimage heads; `GATE_PLACE_FINISH` already uses framed bytes. Other place-key signatures currently reach beyond floor scope: KEL inception, place card, `leaf-identity.ts:57` place proof, plus calls through `loadVesselSigningSeed` for relay/daemon/internal asks and device/face operations (`open-node-vessel.ts:981,1447,2283,3451`). X0 must sever those duties to named keys/face atoms, not bless them into the floor allowlist. Browser custody census says no browser S key today (`K15:105–115`); browser seed consumers still require parity audit. I found browser UI reads/displays vessel key (`lararium-web/main.ts:239`) but no browser place-KEL author/reader in the named web surface: absence reads as a readiness gap, not proof that no indirect bundle consumer exists.

Road:544 bakes signer-generated `len(D) ‖ D ‖ preimage`, signer and all verifiers together. It changes signed bytes for four duties (carrier, pin, pointer, raise); gate-place-finish’s current prefix already matches the same framing shape, but its preimage construction must avoid double framing. Exact-head checks retire as part of bake; no dual mode/back-compat.

## ⏿ Orient — LEAD model and stories

One KEL format across house identity relations best honors “one name, one relation” and reuses tested fold logic; place KEL gets its own authority rule and duty boundary. KERI supports this shape, but its witness/quorum infrastructure does not follow automatically: house causality remains local-first, no global now. A reader trusts its last verified head, reports “as of my last sync,” and treats two successors from one head as a fork, never choosing by arrival order.

**Gaia theft:** a family loses a laptop with current online signer. A separate cold founding card holds the committed next key. Operator presents it, verifies the current KEL head, signs rotation revealing committed key and fresh commitment, retires old signer, provisions a new cold card. Peers that see descent accept it; offline peer still holds prior head until it sees the chain. If thief also stole card, re-found and re-carriage/re-vouch; old signed history cannot establish the new place.

**Elyncia waystone:** a herm carries its shrine offline while the place rotates. Herm keeps opaque event bytes and old verified head; it never reads or resolves them. On return, a reader verifies each link from its own prior head. Valid descent updates its pin association; missing events leave it at last known head; fork leaves it at common ancestor and asks for operator adjudication/re-found evidence. No remote “latest” or wall-clock freshness claim.

## ◇ Decide — readiness kit, red first

1. **KEL and custody foundation (X0 member readiness red):** mesh place KEL model/event digest/fold, node and browser readers/writers, founding card’s offline next-key generation/read-back, signer storage and rotation ceremony. Red: current-key thief cannot forge committed reveal; wrong reveal, replay, seq gap, prior mismatch, fork all refuse/surface. Control: current valid key signs duty. Positive: valid pre-rotated descendant verifies identically in node/browser. Keep legacy exact inception out; X0 fresh genesis only.
2. **Scoped signer/framing (X0 readiness red):** allowlist five duties; move KEL signing to card key; split all other current seed reach to proper face/daemon/relay keys. Update every signer/verifier together. Red: arbitrary payload/domain confusion, replay across duty, old unframed signature, overscope signing. Positive known-valid signature per each duty under framed bytes. Each verifier consumes only framing and its payload.
3. **Custody and recovery seam:** public KEL/card in F, named online signer floor row outside node S and FCK; card-held next key never copied into online image/log/environment. Red: disk-only attacker cannot derive S/FCK or future key; secret scan/output and restart tests; positive unattended Herm duties. Open owner-choice: host-protection fallback failure and explicit re-found/re-vouch steps.
4. **Cross-relation witness:** Gaia theft and offline herm scenarios across node/browser, restart/reorder/replay. Silent-zero paired with positive rotation and duty sign. Herm carriage stays byte-opaque.

**Files held:** mesh `vessel-kel.ts`, new place-KEL core, `place-signer.ts`, each five duty byte builder/reader; node `node-vessel-identity.ts`, `vault-carriers.ts`, `open-node-vessel.ts`, founding/card and custody; browser `packages/lararium-browser` custody/open path and `lararium-web` display/read path. **Overlap:** K17-R owns lease law, realm boards, frontier verifier—consume its authority results, do not mix realm/lease events into place KEL. W28 owns `gate-exchange`, `face-gate*`, `face-dial`—coordinate gate-place-finish framing at shared boundary; do not independently alter exchange semantics. W26 floor release/content lineage remains separate.

## ▶ Act — operator questions sorted

(a) **Canon:** “The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL” (road:207); “the next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter’s cold next key, offline” (road:207); “the floor key commits no next key” (road:363); X0 “signer frames each duty’s domain itself” and all five matchers retire together (road:544). X0 builds nothing; each member carries readiness red.

(b) **House-law derivations:** no global now → last verified head only; build end state → one X0 bake, no interim mode; one name/relation → place authority distinct from persona, charter, floor-release; herm carries/never reads → opaque events; isomorphic-by-intent → node/browser same verifier vectors; silent-zero/known-positive → paired empty and positive controls; evergreen → fixed causal format, no time; shared act never shared secret → operator act can authorize, never transports the card secret.

(c) **Truly operator-owned:** card replacement/loss procedure and whether old AID re-founds (lean: re-found if card plus signer compromised); online signer at-rest fallback when TPM absent (lean: owner-only protected file, never environment); who may invoke rotation (lean: explicit human ceremony using cold card, unattended vessel may publish only already authorized event bytes); bootstrap/re-vouch route after total loss (lean: operator presents a new genesis and peers treat it as new AID, then re-carriage). K15 explicitly leaves exact signer protection/restart behavior open; do not call the lean canon.

No repo edits, git writes, or suites. The place-KEL lane has a ruled shape and custody model, with per-member X0 reds and the named operator seams above.

